<!--
doc/src/sgml/ref/cypher_call.sgml
PostgreSQL documentation
-->

<refentry id="cypher-call">
 <indexterm zone="cypher-call">
  <primary>CALL</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>CALL</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>Cypher - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>CALL</refname>
  <refpurpose>run a routine or a subquery inside a query pipeline</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
[ OPTIONAL ] CALL <replaceable class="parameter">function</replaceable> ( [ <replaceable class="parameter">argument</replaceable> [, <replaceable class="parameter">argument</replaceable> ] ... ] ) YIELD *
[ OPTIONAL ] CALL <replaceable class="parameter">function</replaceable> ( [ <replaceable class="parameter">argument</replaceable> [, <replaceable class="parameter">argument</replaceable> ] ... ] ) YIELD <replaceable class="parameter">column</replaceable> [ AS <replaceable class="parameter">alias</replaceable> ] [, <replaceable class="parameter">column</replaceable> [ AS <replaceable class="parameter">alias</replaceable> ] ] ...

[ OPTIONAL ] CALL { <replaceable class="parameter">subquery</replaceable> }
[ OPTIONAL ] CALL ( ) { <replaceable class="parameter">subquery</replaceable> }
[ OPTIONAL ] CALL ( <replaceable class="parameter">variable</replaceable> [, <replaceable class="parameter">variable</replaceable> ] ... ) { <replaceable class="parameter">subquery</replaceable> }
[ OPTIONAL ] CALL ( * ) { <replaceable class="parameter">subquery</replaceable> }
[ OPTIONAL ] CALL { WITH <replaceable class="parameter">variable</replaceable> [, <replaceable class="parameter">variable</replaceable> ] ... <replaceable class="parameter">subquery</replaceable> }
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-call-description">
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   <command>CALL</command> runs a routine from inside a query pipeline. The token after
   the keyword decides which form you get. A name is the named routine form: it invokes a
   function and adds the columns named in the mandatory <literal>YIELD</literal> list to
   the working row. A <literal>(</literal> or a <literal>{</literal> is the subquery form:
   it runs the query written between the braces.
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>OPTIONAL</literal> may prefix either form. It keeps an input row whose routine
   or subquery produced no rows, binding to null everything the call would have returned.
  </para>

  <refsect2 id="cypher-call-what-it-invokes">
   <title>What CALL Invokes</title>

   <para>
    In GQL the thing <command>CALL</command> invokes is a procedure. AgensGraph has no
    procedure catalog of its own. <literal>CALL ... YIELD</literal> resolves the name
    against ordinary PostgreSQL functions, and a stock installation registers no
    procedures at all. Any function works, and its shape decides what
    <literal>YIELD</literal> can name.
   </para>

   <variablelist>
    <varlistentry>
     <term>A scalar function, such as <literal>length(text)</literal></term>
     <listitem>
      <para>One column, named after the function.</para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>

    <varlistentry>
     <term>A set returning function, such as <literal>generate_series(int, int)</literal></term>
     <listitem>
      <para>One column named after the function, and one row per element.</para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>

    <varlistentry>
     <term>A composite or <literal>RETURNS TABLE</literal> function, such as
      <literal>jsonb_each_text(jsonb)</literal></term>
     <listitem>
      <para>One column per output field, under the field names.</para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>

    <varlistentry>
     <term>Any of these, named by the function itself</term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       The whole row, when no output column carries that name. A function declared
       <literal>RETURNS SETOF vertex</literal> yields vertices this way.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>
   </variablelist>

   <para>
    A yield item is one of these and nothing else. A name the function does not return is
    refused, even when a variable of that name is bound earlier in the query.
   </para>

   <para>
    PostgreSQL's own <command>CALL</command> statement is a different thing. It is a top
    level SQL statement, it requires a real procedure, and it is not a Cypher clause. The
    two do not overlap, and each rejects the other's target.
   </para>
  </refsect2>

  <refsect2 id="cypher-call-named-form">
   <title>The Named Routine Form</title>

   <para>
    The clause becomes a function scan joined onto the rows the preceding clause produced,
    like a lateral <literal>SELECT * FROM function(arguments)</literal>. The
    <literal>YIELD</literal> list picks from that result. A yield item is resolved against
    the function's result and nothing else.
   </para>

   <itemizedlist>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <literal>YIELD</literal> is mandatory. <literal>CALL f(x)</literal> with no
      <literal>YIELD</literal> is a syntax error.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      The clause can neither open a query nor end one. <literal>CALL</literal> at the start
      of a statement is read as PostgreSQL's SQL <command>CALL</command>, so a leading
      <literal>CALL f() YIELD ...</literal> is a syntax error.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      When the arguments reference variables bound earlier the call is correlated and runs
      once per input row. With constant arguments the function runs once and its rows cross
      join onto the pipeline.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Yielded columns are ordinary bindings afterwards. Filter them, aggregate them, order
      by them, or feed them to a following <xref linkend="cypher-match"/>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      An input row for which the function yields no rows is dropped, exactly as an inner
      join drops it. <literal>OPTIONAL</literal> keeps it.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </itemizedlist>
  </refsect2>

  <refsect2 id="cypher-call-subquery-form">
   <title>The Subquery Form</title>

   <para>
    <literal>CALL { ... }</literal> evaluates the subquery and joins its result onto the
    working table. The import list decides entirely what the body can see. A bare
    <literal>CALL { ... }</literal> imports nothing. A pattern inside the braces declares
    its variable names, so an outer variable's name written in the body binds a fresh
    variable of the same name rather than referring to the outer one. Name the variables
    the body needs.
   </para>

   <variablelist>
    <varlistentry>
     <term><literal>CALL { ... }</literal></term>
     <listitem><para>The body sees nothing.</para></listitem>
    </varlistentry>
    <varlistentry>
     <term><literal>CALL ( ) { ... }</literal></term>
     <listitem><para>The body sees nothing.</para></listitem>
    </varlistentry>
    <varlistentry>
     <term><literal>CALL (v, w) { ... }</literal></term>
     <listitem><para>The body sees <literal>v</literal> and <literal>w</literal>.</para></listitem>
    </varlistentry>
    <varlistentry>
     <term><literal>CALL (*) { ... }</literal></term>
     <listitem><para>The body sees every variable in scope.</para></listitem>
    </varlistentry>
    <varlistentry>
     <term><literal>CALL { WITH v, w ... }</literal></term>
     <listitem><para>The body sees <literal>v</literal> and <literal>w</literal>.</para></listitem>
    </varlistentry>
   </variablelist>

   <para>
    A scope clause and an importing <literal>WITH</literal> may be combined.
    <literal>CALL (v) { WITH w ... }</literal> imports both. An import list is a plain list
    of names in either spelling, and <literal>AS</literal> is not accepted in it. Three
    near misses are syntax errors rather than alternatives:
    <literal>CALL (*) { WITH v ... }</literal>, <literal>CALL (*, v) { ... }</literal>, and
    an importing <literal>WITH</literal> that is not a plain list of names, which rules out
    both <literal>WITH *</literal> and <literal>WITH v AS x</literal>.
   </para>

   <para>
    With an import list the body runs once per input row, with those variables bound, and
    the rows it returns are joined to that row. With no imports the body is uncorrelated:
    it runs once and its result cross joins onto every input row. An input row for which
    the body returns nothing is dropped, and <literal>OPTIONAL</literal> keeps it.
   </para>

   <para>
    The body is read only and must end with <xref linkend="cypher-return"/>. Write clauses
    are rejected at parse time, as a syntax error at the write keyword, one for each of
    <literal>CREATE</literal>, <literal>INSERT</literal>, <literal>MERGE</literal>,
    <literal>SET</literal>, <literal>REMOVE</literal>, <literal>DELETE</literal> and
    <literal>DETACH DELETE</literal>. A body ending in <literal>FINISH</literal> raises
    <literal>CALL subquery must RETURN at least one variable</literal>.
   </para>

   <para>
    The names the body returns become bindings in the enclosing query. A name already bound
    outside is rejected rather than shadowed, with
    <literal>variable "name" returned by the CALL subquery is already bound in the outer
    query</literal>. Alias it in the body's <literal>RETURN</literal>. The body may not bind
    one output name twice either, and neither may a <literal>YIELD</literal> list.
   </para>

   <para>
    A binding inside the body that would hide an outer name is refused as well. That covers
    a <xref linkend="cypher-let"/>, an <xref linkend="cypher-unwind"/>, a
    <xref linkend="cypher-for"/>, a renaming <xref linkend="cypher-with"/> and a nested
    <command>CALL</command> whose result carries the name. Imported variables keep their
    names for the whole body.
   </para>

   <para>
    The body may use <command>MATCH</command>, <literal>OPTIONAL MATCH</literal>,
    <command>WITH</command>, <command>LET</command>, <command>UNWIND</command>,
    <command>FOR</command>, <command>LOAD FROM</command>, a nested <command>CALL</command>,
    <literal>DISTINCT</literal>, <literal>ORDER BY</literal>, <literal>SKIP</literal>,
    <literal>LIMIT</literal> and the set operators. <xref linkend="cypher-next"/> is not
    accepted. Neither is a SQL <literal>SELECT</literal>: the body is a Cypher read
    statement. <literal>CALL ... PER (...)</literal> is not supported. How often the body
    runs is decided by the import list alone.
   </para>
  </refsect2>

  <refsect2 id="cypher-call-optional">
   <title>OPTIONAL</title>

   <para>
    A plain <command>CALL</command> joins the call's rows onto the pipeline, so an input row
    that produced none drops out. <literal>OPTIONAL CALL</literal> lowers to a left join
    instead, which keeps that row. The result is one row, carrying the input row's own
    values, with every variable the call would have bound set to null.
   </para>

   <itemizedlist>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Preservation is decided per input row. A correlated call that finds nothing for one
      row nulls only that row and leaves the others alone.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Every variable the call would have bound is nulled together, whatever its type:
      vertex, edge, path, list, map or scalar.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      The binding is a real SQL null, not a jsonb null, so <literal>IS NULL</literal> is the
      way to test it.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <literal>OPTIONAL</literal> preserves rows, it does not invent them. An empty input
      still produces no output.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      A preserved row is an ordinary row afterwards, so a later clause multiplies it like
      any other.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </itemizedlist>

   <para>
    <literal>OPTIONAL</literal> relaxes nothing else. A write clause in the body is still a
    syntax error, a body that returns an outer name is still rejected, and a bare
    <literal>OPTIONAL CALL { ... }</literal> still imports nothing.
   </para>

   <para>
    Some bodies always produce at least one row, and for those <literal>OPTIONAL</literal>
    changes nothing. An ungrouped aggregate is the common case: a person with no credits
    gets <literal>0</literal>, not null.
   </para>
  </refsect2>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-call-parameters">
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">function</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Name of the function to invoke, schema qualified if <varname>search_path</varname>
      does not reach it. The parentheses are required even with no arguments. A procedure is
      refused with <literal>name() is a procedure</literal> and the hint
      <literal>To call a procedure, use CALL</literal>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">argument</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      A value expression, evaluated per input row. An argument that references a variable
      bound earlier makes the call correlated. A property access produces
      <type>jsonb</type>, so a function whose parameter is another type needs an explicit
      cast.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">column</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      An output column of the function, or the function's own name for the whole row.
      <literal>YIELD *</literal> takes all of them. Any other name is refused with
      <literal>function f has no output column "x"</literal>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">alias</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      New name for the yielded column. It is required when the column's own name is already
      bound in the query, because a yielded name that collides is rejected rather than
      shadowing: <literal>variable "m" yielded by CALL is already bound in the outer
      query</literal>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">variable</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      A variable bound in the enclosing query, made visible inside the braces. One that does
      not exist raises <literal>variable "name" to import into CALL does not exist</literal>.
      A scope clause with no preceding clause raises
      <literal>CALL has no preceding clause to import variables from</literal>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">subquery</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      A read only Cypher query ending in <command>RETURN</command>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-call-examples">
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   These examples use the graph in <xref linkend="agensgraph-example-data"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   Expand one film's properties into rows. <literal>jsonb_each_text</literal> returns two
   columns, and <literal>YIELD</literal> names both:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'The Matrix'})
CALL jsonb_each_text(jsonb_build_object('title', m.title, 'year', m.year, 'genre', m.genre))
     YIELD key, value
RETURN key AS property, value ORDER BY property;

 property |    value
----------+--------------
 "genre"  | "Action"
 "title"  | "The Matrix"
 "year"   | "1999"
(3 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>AS</literal> renames a yielded column for every clause that follows. Yielding a
   subset is allowed, so <literal>value</literal> is not bound here:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'The Matrix'})
CALL jsonb_each_text(properties(m)) YIELD key AS property
RETURN property ORDER BY property;

  property
-------------
 "embedding"
 "genre"
 "plot"
 "title"
 "year"
(5 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>YIELD *</literal> takes every column. The arguments here are constants, so
   <literal>generate_series</literal> runs once and its two rows cross join onto the film:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie) WHERE m.genre = 'Adventure'
CALL generate_series(1, 2) YIELD *
RETURN m.title AS film, generate_series AS n ORDER BY n;

      film       | n
-----------------+---
 "Jurassic Park" | 1
 "Jurassic Park" | 2
(2 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Pass a value from the row and the function runs per row. <literal>m.year</literal> is
   <type>jsonb</type>, so it needs a cast:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'The Matrix'})
CALL generate_series(1, (m.year)::int) YIELD generate_series AS n
RETURN count(n) AS years;

 years
-------
 1999
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Without the cast the function cannot be resolved:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'The Matrix'})
CALL generate_series(1, m.year) YIELD generate_series AS n
RETURN count(n);

ERROR:  function generate_series(integer, jsonb) does not exist
LINE 2: CALL generate_series(1, m.year) YIELD generate_series AS n
        ^
HINT:  No function matches the given name and argument types. You might need to add explicit type casts.
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   A second call may correlate on the first one's yielded column:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'The Matrix'})
CALL generate_series(1, 2) YIELD generate_series AS n
CALL generate_series(1, n) YIELD generate_series AS k
RETURN n, k ORDER BY n, k;

 n | k
---+---
 1 | 1
 2 | 1
 2 | 2
(3 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   When no output column carries the function's own name, that name stands for the whole
   row:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'The Matrix'})
CALL jsonb_each_text(jsonb_build_object('title', m.title, 'year', m.year))
     YIELD jsonb_each_text AS pair
RETURN pair ORDER BY pair;

                  pair
-----------------------------------------
 {"key": "title", "value": "The Matrix"}
 {"key": "year", "value": "1999"}
(2 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   The subquery form needs its import list. Written without one, this query answers a
   different question. Every film reports the same cast size, which is the number of
   <literal>acted_in</literal> edges in the whole graph:
<programlisting>
MATCH (d:person)-[:directed]-&gt;(m:movie)
CALL { MATCH (a:person)-[:acted_in]-&gt;(m) RETURN count(a) AS cast_size }
RETURN m.title AS film, cast_size ORDER BY film LIMIT 4;

        film         | cast_size
---------------------+-----------
 "Avatar"            | 25
 "Blade Runner"      | 25
 "Blade Runner 2049" | 25
 "Elysium"           | 25
(4 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   The <literal>m</literal> inside the braces is not the <literal>m</literal> matched
   outside. It is a new variable that the body's own pattern declares, constrained only by
   the <literal>acted_in</literal> edge. Naming the import asks the question that was
   intended. <literal>CALL (*) { ... }</literal> and <literal>CALL { WITH m ... }</literal>
   give the same numbers:
<programlisting>
MATCH (d:person)-[:directed]-&gt;(m:movie)
CALL (m) { MATCH (a:person)-[:acted_in]-&gt;(m) RETURN count(a) AS cast_size }
RETURN m.title AS film, cast_size ORDER BY film LIMIT 4;

        film         | cast_size
---------------------+-----------
 "Avatar"            | 1
 "Blade Runner"      | 1
 "Blade Runner 2049" | 2
 "Elysium"           | 1
(4 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Nothing is reported for the first query because only expressions reference a variable,
   while patterns declare one. The same mistake inside a <xref linkend="cypher-where"/> is
   caught:
<programlisting>
MATCH (d:person)-[:directed]-&gt;(m:movie)
CALL { MATCH (a:person) WHERE a.name = m.title RETURN count(a) AS n }
RETURN n;

ERROR:  variable does not exist
LINE 2: CALL { MATCH (a:person) WHERE a.name = m.title RETURN count(...
                                               ^
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   An aggregating body returns exactly one row for every input row, even when the value it
   computes is zero, so the outer row count is unchanged:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person) WHERE p.name IN ['Keanu Reeves', 'Christopher Nolan']
CALL (p) { MATCH (p)-[:acted_in]-&gt;(m:movie) RETURN count(m) AS films }
RETURN p.name AS person, films ORDER BY person;

       person        | films
---------------------+-------
 "Christopher Nolan" | 0
 "Keanu Reeves"      | 4
(2 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   An empty scope clause states plainly that the body is uncorrelated. Write it when the
   body is a constant of the query:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person {name: 'Keanu Reeves'})
CALL () { MATCH (m:movie) RETURN count(m) AS movies }
RETURN p.name AS person, movies;

     person     | movies
----------------+--------
 "Keanu Reeves" | 18
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Every named variable is visible, and the body may use only some of them:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person {name: 'Keanu Reeves'}), (q:person {name: 'Carrie-Anne Moss'})
CALL (p, q) { MATCH (p)-[:acted_in]-&gt;(m:movie)&lt;-[:acted_in]-(q) RETURN count(m) AS shared }
RETURN shared;

 shared
--------
 2
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Keanu Reeves has four acting credits, Ryan Gosling one and Christopher Nolan none.
   <literal>OPTIONAL CALL</literal> keeps the director:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person) WHERE p.name IN ['Keanu Reeves', 'Ryan Gosling', 'Christopher Nolan']
OPTIONAL CALL (p) { MATCH (p)-[:acted_in]-&gt;(m:movie) RETURN m.title AS film }
RETURN p.name AS person, film ORDER BY person, film;

       person        |            film
---------------------+----------------------------
 "Christopher Nolan" |
 "Keanu Reeves"      | "The Matrix"
 "Keanu Reeves"      | "The Matrix Reloaded"
 "Keanu Reeves"      | "The Matrix Resurrections"
 "Keanu Reeves"      | "The Matrix Revolutions"
 "Ryan Gosling"      | "Blade Runner 2049"
(6 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Drop the <literal>OPTIONAL</literal> and the same body loses that row entirely, while the
   other two people are unaffected:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person) WHERE p.name IN ['Keanu Reeves', 'Ryan Gosling', 'Christopher Nolan']
CALL (p) { MATCH (p)-[:acted_in]-&gt;(m:movie) RETURN m.title AS film }
RETURN p.name AS person, film ORDER BY person, film;

     person     |            film
----------------+----------------------------
 "Keanu Reeves" | "The Matrix"
 "Keanu Reeves" | "The Matrix Reloaded"
 "Keanu Reeves" | "The Matrix Resurrections"
 "Keanu Reeves" | "The Matrix Revolutions"
 "Ryan Gosling" | "Blade Runner 2049"
(5 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>OPTIONAL</literal> sits in the same place on the named form.
   <literal>generate_series(1, 0)</literal> is empty, so Avatar's row survives with
   <literal>n</literal> null:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie) WHERE m.title IN ['The Matrix', 'Avatar']
OPTIONAL CALL generate_series(1, CASE WHEN m.title = 'Avatar' THEN 0 ELSE 2 END)
         YIELD generate_series AS n
RETURN m.title AS film, n ORDER BY film, n;

     film     | n
--------------+---
 "Avatar"     |
 "The Matrix" | 1
 "The Matrix" | 2
(3 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Every variable the body would have returned is null on a preserved row, and it is a SQL
   null:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person {name: 'Christopher Nolan'})
OPTIONAL CALL (p) { MATCH (p)-[:acted_in]-&gt;(m:movie) RETURN m.title AS film, m.year AS year }
RETURN film IS NULL AS film_is_null, year IS NULL AS year_is_null;

 film_is_null | year_is_null
--------------+--------------
 t            | t
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   With no input row there is nothing to preserve:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person {name: 'Nobody'})
OPTIONAL CALL (p) { MATCH (p)-[:acted_in]-&gt;(m:movie) RETURN m.title AS film }
RETURN p.name AS person, film;

 person | film
--------+------
(0 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-call-notes">
  <title>Notes</title>

  <para>
   The lexer decides between the two forms by looking one token ahead, at the token after
   <literal>call</literal>. A label keeps the name an ordinary identifier, so
   <literal>MATCH (call:movie) RETURN count(call)</literal> still works, and so does the SQL
   statement <literal>CALL myproc()</literal>.
  </para>

  <para>
   A parenthesis or a brace after the name does not. <literal>call(21)</literal> and
   <literal>(call { ... })</literal> both read as the start of a <command>CALL</command> clause
   and are syntax errors, and this reaches plain SQL as well: a function named
   <literal>call</literal> can no longer be created or invoked unquoted from either language.
   Double quote the name in every one of those positions, as
   <literal>"call"(21)</literal> and <literal>("call" { ... })</literal>.
  </para>

  <para>
   The named routine form is a middle clause: it cannot open a query and cannot end one. The
   subquery form may open a query, but only without a scope clause.
  </para>

  <para>
   A procedure is the one routine kind <literal>CALL ... YIELD</literal> will not take:
<programlisting>
CREATE PROCEDURE noop() LANGUAGE sql AS 'SELECT 1';
MATCH (m:movie) CALL noop() YIELD * RETURN count(*);

CREATE PROCEDURE

ERROR:  noop() is a procedure
LINE 1: MATCH (m:movie) CALL noop() YIELD * RETURN count(*);
                        ^
HINT:  To call a procedure, use CALL.
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   A yield item that is not an output column of the function is refused even when a variable
   of that name is bound earlier. The earlier binding is never what <literal>YIELD</literal>
   means:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'The Matrix'})
CALL generate_series(1, 2) YIELD m AS n
RETURN n;

ERROR:  function generate_series has no output column "m"
LINE 2: CALL generate_series(1, 2) YIELD m AS n
                                         ^
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   A function returning the pseudo type <type>record</type> cannot be called. The clause has
   nowhere to put the column definition list PostgreSQL requires, so
   <literal>CALL jsonb_to_recordset(...) YIELD *</literal> raises
   <literal>a column definition list is required for functions returning "record"</literal>.
   Wrap such a call in a SQL subquery instead.
  </para>

  <para>
   The plan shows which execution shape you got. A correlated call is a
   <literal>Nested Loop</literal> whose inner side is the function scan, and
   <literal>OPTIONAL</literal> makes it a <literal>Nested Loop Left Join</literal> with the
   function scan on the nullable side:
<programlisting>
EXPLAIN (COSTS OFF)
MATCH (m:movie) CALL generate_series(1, (m.year)::int) YIELD generate_series AS n
RETURN m.title, n;

               QUERY PLAN
----------------------------------------
 Nested Loop
   -&gt;  Seq Scan on movie m
   -&gt;  Function Scan on generate_series
(3 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   A correlated subquery body is not always a per row subquery in the plan. The planner
   pulls up a body it can flatten, so a body of plain patterns plans as the same join an
   inline pattern would produce. Only a body the planner cannot flatten, such as an
   aggregating one, stays a per row lateral join.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-call-compatibility">
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   <command>CALL</command> implements the GQL call procedure statement and procedure call of
   ISO/IEC 39075:2024 section 15.1, in the query position of section 14.5. Both procedure
   call forms the standard defines are accepted: the inline call of section 15.2 and the
   named call of section 15.3, each with the optional <literal>OPTIONAL</literal> prefix.
   The <literal>YIELD</literal> list follows section 16.14, including syntax rule 7, which
   says that a yield item names a column of the procedure's result, and syntax rule 6, which
   refuses a duplicate alias.
  </para>

  <para>
   The call data modifying procedure statement of section 13.6 is not implemented. A called
   routine or subquery may not write to the graph. Neither is a catalog of GQL procedures: a
   procedure reference of section 17.5 resolves to a PostgreSQL function, and the standard's
   <literal>QUERY PROCEDURE</literal> indication has no counterpart here.
   <literal>CALL ... PER (...)</literal> is not accepted.
  </para>

  <para>
   There are two deviations. Section 15.3 makes the <literal>YIELD</literal> clause optional
   on a named call, and AgensGraph requires it, because the clause has to contribute columns
   to the working table. Section 15.2 syntax rule 3 says that an absent variable scope clause
   is rewritten as a scope clause listing every field of the incoming working record, which
   makes a bare <literal>CALL { ... }</literal> import everything under the standard.
   AgensGraph treats an absent scope clause as importing nothing, like
   <literal>CALL ( ) { ... }</literal>.
  </para>

  <para>
   AgensGraph goes beyond the standard in two places. <literal>(*)</literal> is not in the
   standard's binding variable reference list, and neither is the importing
   <literal>WITH</literal>. Both come from openCypher. And because a name resolves to a
   PostgreSQL function, <literal>CALL ... YIELD</literal> may invoke any PostgreSQL table
   function, not only a graph routine: a set returning function, a composite returning
   function and a function declared <literal>RETURNS TABLE</literal> all work. A SQL
   <literal>SELECT</literal> is not accepted as a <command>CALL</command> body. It is
   accepted as the body of a subquery expression, for which see
   <xref linkend="cypher-subqueryexpressions"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   openCypher writes the named clause the same way but resolves the name against a procedure
   registry, such as <literal>db.labels()</literal> or <literal>dbms.components()</literal>,
   and allows <literal>CALL proc()</literal> with no <literal>YIELD</literal> as a standalone
   statement. Neither exists here. There is no <literal>db</literal> or
   <literal>dbms</literal> namespace, and <literal>YIELD</literal> is required. Read the
   label catalogs instead.
  </para>

  <para>
   openCypher requires the same import list on the subquery form, but it reports the absence
   of one: referring to an undeclared outer variable inside
   <literal>CALL { }</literal> is an error there, not a silent rebinding. Code moved from
   openCypher keeps working, because <literal>CALL (v) { }</literal>,
   <literal>CALL (*) { }</literal> and <literal>CALL { WITH v ... }</literal> all mean what
   they do there. A bare <literal>CALL { }</literal> that openCypher rejected will run here
   and answer differently.
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>OPTIONAL CALL</literal> is a GQL construct. openCypher has only
   <literal>OPTIONAL MATCH</literal>. The two agree wherever both can express the same thing,
   because the standard defines <literal>OPTIONAL MATCH p</literal> as an
   <literal>OPTIONAL CALL</literal> over a body that matches <literal>p</literal>.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

